<p><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> did not have a smartphone. In fact, he didn’t have a phone of any kind, owing to the fact that he lived in the second century, though we can be reasonably certain that he commanded more than enough messengers to keep himself informed around the clock. That’s because he was the Roman emperor at a time when the Roman Empire was at its height. And so, none of us will ever know Marcus’ freedom from digital distraction, nor will we know the intense responsibility he must have felt, no matter how grand a title we attain in our own careers. Still, even here in the twenty-first century, we would all do well to live by the example he left.</p>
<p>So believes Ryan Holiday, a popularizer of <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2017/06/an-animated-introduction-to-stoicism-the-ancient-greek-philosophy-that-lets-you-lead-a-happy-fulfilling-life.html">Stoicism</a>, the school of thought primarily defined by Marcus’ writings, as well as those of statesman-dramatist <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seneca_the_Younger">Seneca the Younger</a> and former slave <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epictetus">Epictetus</a>. In his&nbsp;<a href="https://amzn.to/4wHRZ4Y"><em>Meditations</em></a>, Marcus included enough detail about his own life that Holiday could make <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6K3wiD6ACWg">the video above</a> for his YouTube channel <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@DailyStoic">Daily Stoic</a>, which breaks down the emperor’s daily routine.</p>
