</div>

<p>He rose at dawn, but not out of natural inclination. He did his reflection, including a realistic adjustment of his expectations for the day, before the siege of duties and requests began. He defined time for what Cal Newport, Holiday’s colleague in the smarter regions of digital-era self-help, would call <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2017/08/the-case-for-deleting-your-social-media-accounts.html">deep work</a>.</p>
<p>As with any popularization, Holiday conveys Marcus’ life and thought in terms the man himself wouldn’t recognize, even if they were translated into Latin. One fashionable expression he doesn’t use but could have is “work-life balance.” “Marcus was not all about business,” Holiday says. “We know that he was active in physical exercise. We know that he enjoyed boxing and wrestling and hunting and horseback riding. We know that he would <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2024/01/how-walking-fosters-creativity-stanford-researchers-confirm-what-philosophers-writers-have-always-known.html">take walks</a>.” He would also go to the Colosseum, though without hiding his lack of interest in gladiatorial combat. If he spent more time reading and thinking in his prime seat than getting into the spirit of the games, why attend at all? Perhaps he thought in the manner of <a href="https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/martial">Martial</a>, a poet of an earlier generation whose sentiments would nevertheless ring true with Stoics ancient or modern: “These are my times, and I must know them.”</p>
